Lichen patch for ESP-IDF v6.1 (lichenos-why2025-badge #121): the PSRAM speed, 80 or 120 MHz, is chosen at PSRAM init instead
of at compile time.

Applies ON TOP OF 0001-esp32p4-psram-120mhz.patch (same IDF checkout; 0002 is the alternative to 0001 and not used with this).
Adds CONFIG_SPIRAM_SPEED_RUNTIME_SELECT (depends on SPIRAM_SPEED_80M, default n). With it off nothing changes. With it on,
esp_psram_impl_enable() first calls mspi_timing_psram_speed_select(): it reads LP_SYSTEM_REG_LP_STORE15; the word
0xC4A1 << 16 | 120 selects 120 MHz (MPLL 480 / 4), anything else 80 MHz (MPLL 320 / 4, exactly the stock 80 MHz setting);
the register is cleared by that read. The running speed is g_mspi_psram_speed_mhz, the raw word found is
g_mspi_psram_carrier_found (esp_private/mspi_timing_tuning.h). The 2nd stage bootloader does not compile
mspi_timing_tuning.c nor init PSRAM, so it is unaffected. The latency codes and the DQS tuning tables are the same for
80 and 120 MHz (0001), so only the MPLL frequency and the clock divider are runtime.

    git -C <idf> apply "$PWD/tools/idf-patches/0003-esp32p4-psram-runtime-80-120.patch"

